net - the network interfaces

  net                    every interface: link, counters, address
  net <iface> up         enable that interface, persistently
  net <iface> down       disable it
  net <iface> dhcp       clear any static claim; DHCP asks
  net <iface> static 192.0.2.50/24 gw 192.0.2.1 dns 192.0.2.1
                         claim an address; gw and dns are optional
  net <iface> join <name> [<key>]
                         join a wireless network; the key is a passphrase
                         of 8 to 63 characters or 64 hex digits, and is
                         left out for an open network
  net <iface> forget     stop joining it
  net scan               every network the radio has heard: channel,
                         signal and security

An interface is named the way the listing prints it ("e1000",
"e1000.1" for a second card of the same kind), by class ("ether",
"wifi"), or by its bus location ("03:00.0"). Class names cover
whichever interfaces no more specific entry claims.

Configuration lives in four slots of the settings store and applies
at once: it outlives the command, and `cfg net` shows what is
recorded. Naming an interface edits the slot that matches it, or
takes a free slot; an emptied match (cfg net if0_match "") frees
its slot again.

With no static claim, an enabled interface asks DHCP as soon as its
cable carries, and prints the address when it arrives. An interface
no slot covers stays down.

A radio scans whenever it is not joined, and the listing says which
channel it is on. Every verb here has the same control in the
Settings window's Network pane and in the bar's network menu, and
`cfg net` shows the slot they all edit.

  net -p <address>       one ARP probe beneath the stack; the next
                         `net` shows which machine answered

Settings, generated from the schema: net

  net.hostname              unset
      letters, digits and hyphens; unset takes vibeee-<address>
  net.if0_match             ether
      a class (ether, wifi), a driver name, a bus location, or unset
  net.if0_enabled           true
      true | false
  net.if0_address           unset
      an address and prefix, a.b.c.d/nn; unset asks DHCP
  net.if0_gateway           unset
      an address, a.b.c.d; unset takes what the lease offers
  net.if0_dns               unset
      up to two addresses, comma separated
  net.if0_ssid              unset
      a network name, up to 32 characters
  net.if0_psk               unset
      a passphrase of 8 to 63 characters, or 64 hex digits
  net.if0_regdomain         conservative
      conservative | fcc | etsi | mkk | unrestricted
  net.if0_txpower           regulatory
      regulatory | max | a number of dBm
